  "cause": "**The tariff announcement that read as a shock on Thursday is being read on Friday as a legal repair job, and the tell is that the numbers are ceilings.** The **US Trade Representative's** proposed duties on fourteen jurisdictions — **China** up to **40 per cent**, **Vietnam** up to **35**, and the EU, Japan, India, Korea, Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia, Cambodia, Singapore, Switzerland and Bangladesh at **20 or 25** — do not take effect until **January or possibly February**, after a consultation period. The BBC's economics editor, **Faisal Islam**, notes that *\"there is something rather tentative about this latest exercise with talk of further negotiations,\"* and quotes USTR **Jamieson Greer** telling CNBC: *\"We expect to have a broad, even a plurilateral negotiation to talk about excess capacity and how to resolve it.\"* There have already been **eight months of process and hearings**, and the published rates are upper limits *\"often up for negotiation\"* — a different instrument from the overnight shock of April 2025. The legal architecture explains the caution. After the **Supreme Court struck down** the emergency basis for the original tariffs earlier this year, the administration is rebuilding on **Section 301** findings and, for over half the Canadian duties, **Section 338** of the 1930s Smoot-Hawley act — two bases, each with its own litigation. **Twenty-five states** have sued to strike down the forced-labour tariffs, and the Senate has a bill to repeal the Section 338 duties. The **Speaker-designate, Hakeem Jeffries**, said the new House would in its first 100 days *\"move aggressively to… repeal the Trump tariffs.\"* China's published **40 per cent** sits above an applied **20 per cent**, the truce rate from last year's Trump–Xi deal, which **expires in mid-January**. Beijing's response was muted: no retaliation, a call to implement the deal, and a dismissal of overcapacity as a *\"false issue.\"* The leverage has weakened with the facts — the US share of China's exports has fallen to around **10 per cent**, from 15 two years ago and 19 in 2018 — and the trade has already rerouted: China's exports in 2026 are down about **8 per cent** on 2024, more than made up for elsewhere, while Canada's exports to the rest of the world have *\"rocketed.\"*",
