  "hinge": "§4:53 states the criterion in four words — **in mutual consent**. It does not forbid a price; it forbids a price imposed by deception or force, and it places consent, not fairness of rate, at the centre. A tariff is by construction non-consensual, which is why the verse does not settle whether 40 per cent is just. What it does settle is which procedure can make a tariff legitimate: not a finding written by the office that wants the rate, and not a ceiling announced as a fact, but a negotiation in which the other side can say no. **First,** publish **applied and ceiling side by side** for every line, because a ceiling presented as a rate misrepresents the policy and poisons the consultation it invites. **Second,** put the **China truce renewal** into the same plurilateral forum as the overcapacity talks, since the mid-January expiry is the only date that actually binds either government. **Third,** write **durability into every deal** — a ratchet, a sunset, a termination clause — because a partner that cannot rely on the US keeping an agreement will demand terms that make the agreement worth less to both sides, and the cost of that mistrust is paid by the importer and the consumer, who are not in the room."
