  "cause": "**Gadi Eisenkot bought the left on Thursday with four ministries, and the price of the right went up the same afternoon.** The former military chief's **Yashar** party and **Yair Golan's** left-wing **Democrats** announced a coalition agreement on Thursday under which the Democrats receive **four ministerial portfolios** — public security (an expanded internal security ministry), labour and social affairs, communications, and culture and sport — plus parliamentary positions. The deal came two days after Golan **froze negotiations** in protest at Yashar's parallel talks with **Benjamin Netanyahu's Likud** over a possible unity government, and only hours after Yashar announced the talks had resumed. Earlier in the week **Avigdor Lieberman's** Israel Our Home had agreed to sign, giving Eisenkot two partners and still no majority. The arithmetic is the story. Israel voted on **27 October**; no bloc won a majority; **President Isaac Herzog** tasked Eisenkot with forming a government and gave him until **6 December**. To reach 61 seats he needs the **Haredi parties**, and **UTJ** reportedly said before Thursday's announcement that it would **veto the appointment of Democrats MK Gilad Kariv as education minister** on the grounds that he is a Reform rabbi — a demand that directly contradicts the deal signed hours later. Meanwhile the legal ground beneath the alternative has shifted: on Wednesday the judges in Netanyahu's corruption trial recommended **dropping Case 4000** and suggested resolving another count by agreement, and the prime minister said the trial *\"must end.\"* In Gaza, Israeli strikes killed **ten people** this week while Washington pressed Netanyahu to proceed with a ceasefire plan, and **Hamas** said it was weighing its response.",
