  "cause": "**The Ebola outbreak in eastern Congo passed twelve thousand confirmed cases on Thursday, and the number that matters more is the one the response cannot reach.** Congo's health ministry reported **12,021 confirmed cases**; nearly half of those infected have died. It is the **fastest-growing Ebola outbreak in history**, the deadliest of Congo's **17** outbreaks, and on track to surpass the West African epidemic that killed **11,000** people between 2014 and 2016. The outbreak was declared in **May** and is caused by the rare **Bundibugyo virus**, for which there is **no approved vaccine or treatment** — which means the entire response consists of isolation, contact tracing and safe burial, three interventions that require reaching people. On Thursday morning fighting resumed between the army and the **Convention pour la révolution populaire** (CRP) in **Iga-Barrière**, a mining town about **25 kilometres north of Bunia**, sending new waves of displaced people toward the city. Bunia, the capital of **Ituri** province, is the principal hub for the Ebola response. The CRP is one of more than **100** armed groups in eastern Congo and was founded last year by **Thomas Lubanga**, convicted by the International Criminal Court in 2012 of recruiting children under 15. **Dr Catherine Smallwood**, the WHO's Ebola incident manager, said this week's fighting had already prevented teams from reaching some communities, disrupted monitoring of exposed contacts and left treatment centres with fewer staff; about **a dozen people**, including **two WHO personnel** and Congolese health officials, were evacuated to Bunia on Tuesday after **three days trapped in a hospital in Akwe** amid intense crossfire. Response teams are also contending with poor roads and violence from residents driven by **mistrust and misinformation**.",
