  "cause": "**A tanker carrying up to seven thousand tonnes of diesel capsized twenty-five nautical miles off Ibiza in the early hours of Thursday, and by Friday morning nobody could say what was in the water.** The **Med Emre**, a **Malta-flagged** chemical and products tanker, was sailing from **Algeciras** to **Barcelona** when rescuers in Valencia received a distress call at about **22:30 local time on Wednesday**. Minutes later the vessel reported a leak, and the crew said it was leaning at **90 degrees**. All **17 crew** were rescued, but **seven** were taken to hospital and one was later pronounced dead; the six receiving treatment reportedly had minor injuries including hypothermia. The ship did not sink completely, as first reported — it remains **partially submerged**, 25 nautical miles (46 km) from Ibiza and 57 from Valencia, with the cause of the capsizing still unclear. Spain sent the pollution-control vessel **Clara Campoamor** to assess the environmental impact. The Balearic president, **Marga Prohens**, wrote that *\"there is still little information available about a possible fuel spill\"* and that *\"the situation is stabilised\"*, with further inspections due on Friday morning. The water it is sitting in is not ordinary water: **Ibiza's marine environment was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1999**, and the tanker is carrying a cargo that disperses rather than congeals — which makes the next 48 hours, and the weather, the decisive variables.",
  "bullets": [
   "**Diesel is not heavy fuel, and the difference changes both the damage and the politics** — up to 7,000 tonnes of a light product that evaporates and disperses, in a semi-enclosed sea; the direct effect is that the acute toxicity is concentrated in the water column rather than on the shoreline; the compound consequence is that a dispersed spill is harder to photograph, easier to under-report and quicker to leave the headlines — so political pressure decays faster than the contamination, and the monitoring that matters most is the monitoring nobody will fund after the cameras leave",
   "**A partially submerged wreck is an unsealed source, and the salvage decision belongs to people the coastline cannot vote for** — the vessel on its side, cargo still aboard, inspections pending; the direct effect is that the source can shift, break up or sink at any point in the next weeks; the compound consequence is that tow, seal or lighter is chosen by the owner, the insurer and the flag state, and the cheapest option is often to let a damaged ship go down in deep water — which converts a surface spill into a seabed one and a visible problem into an invisible liability",
   "**The flag is the instrument that separates the risk from the responsibility** — a Maltese flag, Spanish waters, Spanish ports at both ends, a crew of seventeen whose nationality has not been central to the story; the direct effect is that liability runs through a flag state, an owner, a protection-and-indemnity club and a charterer; the compound consequence is that the coast that bears the damage has no authority over any of them, so the cost of a bad ship is externalised onto a tourism economy and a marine reserve — and the incentive to run an old hull under a convenient flag is exactly the incentive the structure creates",
   "**A visible sheen costs more than the ecological damage, which rewards delay** — an island economy entering its planning season, a UNESCO site, an official statement that the situation is stabilised before the spill is characterised; the direct effect is that bookings react to images, not to concentrations; the compound consequence is that the responsible communicator is punished for saying *\"we do not know yet\"*, so the incentive runs toward a reassuring sentence now and a correction later — and the correction never gets the coverage the reassurance did",
   "**This is a sample, not an accident, and the fleet is the cause** — one tanker on one of the busiest routes in the Mediterranean, a sea that carries a large share of Europe's refined products; the direct effect is that the same hulls, flags, crewing levels and inspection intervals are in use on hundreds of sister routes; the compound consequence is that nothing about this event is unusual except that it happened near a UNESCO site, so the regulatory response will be calibrated to the visibility of the coast rather than to the condition of the fleet"
  ],
  "verse": "…so you make your home planet into a shadow world (hell) and place of existence for a limited time, because you wreak destruction on the appearance (nature) and destroy your world through battles (wars) and hatred as well as through jealousy, through pathological craving for revenge and pathological craving for retaliation and through everything evil.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 7, Verse 25",
  "hinge": "§7:25 locates the cause of a shadow world in **destruction of the appearance of nature** — not in malice, and not in a single catastrophe, but in accumulated practice. That is the correct frame for a capsized tanker: the destruction, if it comes, will have been decided years earlier, in a flag registry, an inspection interval, a crewing level and a freight rate. The verse therefore does not ask what caused this hull to turn over. It asks what makes a fleet like this one economical. **First,** publish the **inspection record, age and classification history** of the Med Emre within the week, because causation will be argued by insurers and the record is the only evidence that can be checked before the file is settled. **Second,** keep a **standing salvage and lightering capability** in the Balearics — booms, pumps and a vessel on contract, not on call — because the first 48 hours are decided by what is already on the water, and a pollution-control ship arriving to *\"assess\"* is an observer, not a response. **Third,** attach **routing and reporting requirements for laden tankers** in the Balearic corridor, with a position report and a casualty notification duty, because a sea that carries this much refined product past a World Heritage site should know what is moving through it before something turns over."
